1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,602
A hand-cranked, gear-driven handheld fan:

2
00:00:03,602 --> 00:00:06,716
no motor, no battery, every part 3D
printed.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:12,341
Stage one: the CAD model, the team's own
assembly.

4
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:19,009
Exploded along its shafts: two shell
halves, a crank, three gears, two shafts,
three

5
00:00:19,009 --> 00:00:20,491
axle pins, a propeller.

6
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:26,468
The crank's fifty-four-tooth gear drives a
twelve-tooth pinion on a compound gear,

7
00:00:26,468 --> 00:00:29,259
whose large gear drives the propeller's
pinion.

8
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:35,643
Two speed-up stages: in theory, about
twenty propeller turns per crank turn.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,049
CAD is where fit, alignment, centre
distance and clearance are checked, before

10
00:00:41,049 --> 00:00:41,632
printing.

11
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:48,816
Stage two: STL export. Each part gets its
own file, as each is printed separately.

12
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,083
The exact CAD surfaces become a mesh of
flat triangles.

13
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:58,747
The mesh is checked: closed, normals
outward, scale in millimetres; then
oriented

14
00:00:58,747 --> 00:00:59,573
for printing.

15
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:04,254
Stage three: slicing. Parts lie flat on
the plate:

16
00:01:04,254 --> 00:01:09,227
shells open side up, gears flat, so each
tooth forms within every layer.

17
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:14,869
The slicer cuts each part into thin layers
and plans every toolpath:

18
00:01:14,869 --> 00:01:16,117
walls, then infill.

19
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:22,465
The report suggests finer layers and extra
walls for gears; supports go only where

20
00:01:22,465 --> 00:01:23,941
an overhang needs them.

21
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:30,748
Stage four: FDM printing. Filament is
melted in a heated nozzle and laid along
the

22
00:01:30,748 --> 00:01:31,691
planned path.

23
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:37,732
The first layer bonds to the bed; the
nozzle then rises one layer, and each
layer

24
00:01:37,732 --> 00:01:38,872
fuses to the last.

25
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:45,339
Nothing is cut away: the gear is built up,
teeth first, then its pinion.

26
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,128
Shell walls grow the same way, leaving
fine layer lines.

27
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:57,771
When the print ends, the bed cools before
parts are removed.

28
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:05,024
Stage five: finishing. A printed part is
not yet a mechanism.

29
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:10,602
Brims and strings are trimmed; gear flanks
and shafts are sanded to remove layer

30
00:02:10,602 --> 00:02:11,040
ridges.

31
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,565
Pin holes are reamed so shafts turn
freely, and the gears are lubricated.

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,733
Balanced on its shaft, the propeller
should stop at random angles.

33
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:28,460
Stage six: assembly and test. The crank
gear, compound gear and propeller pinion
go

34
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:30,096
into the shell, meshing.

35
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,843
The second shell closes the train; the
crank handle, propeller and cap go on
last.

36
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:42,957
By design, the crank drives both gear
stages, so the propeller spins far faster
than

37
00:02:42,957 --> 00:02:43,531
the hand.

38
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:49,452
The test plan: fit, free rotation, speed
ratio, airflow, endurance and balance.

39
00:02:49,452 --> 00:02:51,645
Results are still to be measured.

40
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:55,175
From CAD model to finished product:

41
00:02:55,175 --> 00:02:58,339
one digital design, six stages, no
tooling.
